1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,171
Fall into a small black hole, and you die before you even reach it.

2
00:00:04,171 --> 00:00:09,150
Fall into a giant one, and you cross the point of no return without feeling a thing.

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:12,884
Today we jump into both, and follow you all the way to the end.

4
00:00:12,884 --> 00:00:17,330
Including the strangest part of all: what your friends back home would see.

5
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:19,320
Here's the experiment.

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,258
Two black holes.

7
00:00:20,258 --> 00:00:26,471
The first is small, about ten times the mass of the Sun, the kind left behind when a giant star collapses.

8
00:00:26,471 --> 00:00:35,967
The second is the monster at the center of our own galaxy, Sagittarius A star, about four million times the mass of the Sun, twenty six thousand light years away.

9
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:40,539
By the end, you'll know exactly how much time you'd have left inside each one.

10
00:00:40,539 --> 00:00:43,118
For one of them, it's far less than a blink.

11
00:00:43,818 --> 00:00:46,037
What Happens If You Fall Into a Black Hole?

12
00:00:46,737 --> 00:00:48,642
First, forget the vacuum cleaner.

13
00:00:48,642 --> 00:00:49,969
Black holes don't suck.

14
00:00:49,969 --> 00:00:59,436
If you could crush the Sun into a black hole with the same mass, it would be only about six kilometers wide, and Earth would keep orbiting exactly as it does today.

15
00:00:59,436 --> 00:01:03,189
NASA says it plainly: the orbits of the planets would not change.

16
00:01:03,189 --> 00:01:05,613
It would just get very dark and very cold.

17
00:01:05,613 --> 00:01:09,827
From far away, a black hole pulls like any other object of the same mass.

18
00:01:09,827 --> 00:01:12,251
The danger only starts when you get close.

19
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:14,270
So what counts as close?

20
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:17,238
Every black hole has an edge called the event horizon.

21
00:01:17,238 --> 00:01:18,282
It isn't a surface.

22
00:01:18,282 --> 00:01:19,985
There's nothing there to touch.

23
00:01:19,985 --> 00:01:24,161
It's simply the distance where escaping would mean moving faster than light.

24
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:27,404
Cross it, and nothing, not even light, ever comes back out.

25
00:01:27,404 --> 00:01:31,965
A little farther out is the photon sphere, where gravity bends light into a circle.

26
00:01:31,965 --> 00:01:33,668
Light there can orbit the hole.

27
00:01:33,668 --> 00:01:37,954
In principle, you could look straight ahead and see the back of your own head.

28
00:01:38,654 --> 00:01:40,496
Let's start with the small one.

29
00:01:40,496 --> 00:01:44,476
Ten Suns of mass, squeezed into a ball about sixty kilometers wide.

30
00:01:44,476 --> 00:01:47,328
You could drive across it in well under an hour.

31
00:01:47,328 --> 00:01:48,516
You fall feet first.

32
00:01:48,516 --> 00:01:49,883
And here's the problem.

33
00:01:49,883 --> 00:01:54,754
Gravity pulls harder on your feet than on your head, because your feet are closer.

34
00:01:54,754 --> 00:01:57,606
On Earth, that difference is too tiny to notice.

35
00:01:57,606 --> 00:02:05,448
Here, by our calculation, about eight thousand kilometers from the center, the stretch on your body already equals your full weight.

36
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:08,835
As if a second copy of you were hanging from your ankles.

37
00:02:09,535 --> 00:02:10,877
And it gets worse fast.

38
00:02:10,877 --> 00:02:14,027
Halve the distance, and the stretch grows eight times.

39
00:02:14,027 --> 00:02:18,461
At about seventeen hundred kilometers out, it's a hundred times your weight.

40
00:02:18,461 --> 00:02:19,336
Already deadly.

41
00:02:19,336 --> 00:02:22,953
Physicists really do call what happens next spaghettification.

42
00:02:22,953 --> 00:02:29,545
You're pulled long and thin, and squeezed from the sides, until you're a stream of atoms pouring toward the hole.

43
00:02:30,245 --> 00:02:37,294
By the time what's left of you reaches the event horizon, the stretch is around twenty million times Earth's gravity.

44
00:02:37,294 --> 00:02:41,270
So in a small black hole, nobody crosses the horizon in one piece.

45
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:43,920
You never even get to see the edge up close.

46
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:45,457
Now the giant.

47
00:02:45,457 --> 00:02:46,592
Sagittarius A star.

48
00:02:46,592 --> 00:02:48,922
Four million times the mass of the Sun.

49
00:02:48,922 --> 00:02:51,192
You'd think more mass means more pain.

50
00:02:51,192 --> 00:02:52,268
It's the opposite.

51
00:02:52,268 --> 00:02:59,855
A bigger black hole has a horizon so far from its center that the difference between your head and your feet almost disappears.

52
00:02:59,855 --> 00:03:04,933
Right at the edge, the stretch on your body is less than a thousandth of your weight.

53
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:06,487
You wouldn't feel a thing.

54
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:09,164
This isn't just theory on paper.

55
00:03:09,164 --> 00:03:20,411
In twenty twenty four, NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center ran a supercomputer simulation of exactly this plunge, into a black hole of four point three million Suns, the same as ours.

56
00:03:20,411 --> 00:03:26,962
The virtual camera falls for about three hours, loops around almost twice, and crosses the horizon intact.

57
00:03:26,962 --> 00:03:29,928
The bigger the black hole, the gentler its edge.

58
00:03:30,628 --> 00:03:32,236
But here's the strangest part.

59
00:03:32,236 --> 00:03:34,701
Your friend stays far away, watching you fall.

60
00:03:34,701 --> 00:03:38,452
From your point of view, your watch ticks normally the whole way down.

61
00:03:38,452 --> 00:03:41,292
You cross the horizon at a perfectly ordinary second.

62
00:03:41,292 --> 00:03:43,864
From your friend's point of view, you slow down.

63
00:03:43,864 --> 00:03:47,187
Each tick of your watch takes longer and longer to reach them.

64
00:03:47,187 --> 00:03:50,509
Your light stretches into red, then into infrared, then fades.

65
00:03:50,509 --> 00:03:51,849
They never see you cross.

66
00:03:51,849 --> 00:03:55,332
You seem to freeze at the edge, dimming until you're simply gone.

67
00:03:56,032 --> 00:03:56,954
So who's right?

68
00:03:56,954 --> 00:03:57,692
Both of you.

69
00:03:57,692 --> 00:04:00,274
There is no single clock for the universe.

70
00:04:00,274 --> 00:04:04,331
Gravity bends time, and near a horizon it bends it to the extreme.

71
00:04:04,331 --> 00:04:07,590
NASA's simulation shows this even without falling in.

72
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:17,856
An astronaut who dives close to the horizon and climbs back out, a trip of about six hours, comes home thirty six minutes younger than the partner who waited far away.

73
00:04:18,556 --> 00:04:19,411
Now you cross.

74
00:04:19,411 --> 00:04:20,631
And nothing happens.

75
00:04:20,631 --> 00:04:22,279
No wall, no flash, no sign.

76
00:04:22,279 --> 00:04:24,232
That's what makes it terrifying.

77
00:04:24,232 --> 00:04:27,527
Because inside the horizon, space and time swap roles.

78
00:04:27,527 --> 00:04:31,555
The center, the singularity, is no longer a place in front of you.

79
00:04:31,555 --> 00:04:33,325
It's a moment in your future.

80
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:37,475
Steering away from it would be like steering away from next Tuesday.

81
00:04:37,475 --> 00:04:40,892
Every direction you can possibly move now points inward.

82
00:04:41,592 --> 00:04:42,941
So how long do you get?

83
00:04:42,941 --> 00:04:44,642
Here's the answer I promised.

84
00:04:44,642 --> 00:04:47,516
It depends entirely on which black hole you pick.

85
00:04:47,516 --> 00:04:53,674
In the small one, the trip from the horizon to the center lasts, at most, about a sixth of a millisecond.

86
00:04:53,674 --> 00:04:59,305
A nerve signal from your toes would need around a hundred times longer just to reach your brain.

87
00:04:59,305 --> 00:05:01,005
You would never even feel it.

88
00:05:01,705 --> 00:05:07,655
In Sagittarius A star, our calculation gives you at most about sixty six seconds of your own time.

89
00:05:07,655 --> 00:05:10,448
And firing your rockets only makes it shorter.

90
00:05:10,448 --> 00:05:18,098
In NASA's simulation, the camera lasts twelve point eight seconds after crossing before the stretching finally tears it apart.

91
00:05:18,098 --> 00:05:21,801
About a minute, to see something no one outside can ever see.

92
00:05:22,501 --> 00:05:23,449
Want more time?

93
00:05:23,449 --> 00:05:25,093
You need a bigger monster.

94
00:05:25,093 --> 00:05:26,673
And they get much bigger.

95
00:05:26,673 --> 00:05:31,096
This is how wide the horizon of Sagittarius A star is, drawn to scale.

96
00:05:31,096 --> 00:05:33,498
Around twenty five million kilometers.

97
00:05:33,498 --> 00:05:39,312
Bigger than the Sun eighteen times over, yet it would still fit inside the orbit of Mercury.

98
00:05:40,012 --> 00:05:47,273
Now M eighty seven star, the first black hole ever photographed, by the Event Horizon Telescope in twenty nineteen.

99
00:05:47,273 --> 00:05:52,702
Fifty five million light years away, six and a half billion times the mass of the Sun.

100
00:05:52,702 --> 00:05:56,490
Its horizon is about thirty eight billion kilometers across.

101
00:05:56,490 --> 00:06:01,793
Drop it where our Sun is, and it would swallow the orbit of Pluto, three times over.

102
00:06:02,493 --> 00:06:07,347
Fall into that one, and the clock inside runs for up to about twenty eight hours.

103
00:06:07,347 --> 00:06:08,605
More than a full day.

104
00:06:08,605 --> 00:06:13,459
Enough time to eat, to sleep, maybe to write a letter that no one will ever read.

105
00:06:14,159 --> 00:06:17,375
And none of this is just equations on a blackboard.

106
00:06:17,375 --> 00:06:29,420
In twenty fifteen, the LIGO detectors felt space itself shiver as two black holes, about thirty six and twenty nine times the mass of the Sun, merged one point three billion light years away.

107
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:41,465
Then the Event Horizon Telescope, a network of radio dishes that works like a telescope as wide as the Earth, photographed the glowing rings around M eighty seven star and Sagittarius A star.

108
00:06:41,465 --> 00:06:43,798
A dark shadow, wrapped in bent light.

109
00:06:43,798 --> 00:06:46,131
Just like the drawing on your screen.

110
00:06:46,831 --> 00:06:50,976
And if you'd rather wait outside, black holes don't last forever either.

111
00:06:50,976 --> 00:06:57,595
In nineteen seventy four, Stephen Hawking showed they should slowly leak away energy, now called Hawking radiation.

112
00:06:57,595 --> 00:06:59,437
But slowly is an understatement.

113
00:06:59,437 --> 00:07:05,193
A black hole with the mass of our Sun would take around ten to the sixty seventh years to evaporate.

114
00:07:05,193 --> 00:07:07,668
That's a one followed by sixty seven zeros.

115
00:07:07,668 --> 00:07:10,489
The universe is about fourteen billion years old.

116
00:07:10,489 --> 00:07:11,813
It hasn't even started.

117
00:07:12,513 --> 00:07:14,230
Is there one anywhere near us?

118
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:19,839
The closest black hole we know, Gaia B H one, is about fifteen hundred and sixty light years away.

119
00:07:19,839 --> 00:07:29,626
In twenty twenty four, the European Space Agency's Gaia mission found Gaia B H three, thirty three times the mass of the Sun, about nineteen hundred light years from here.

120
00:07:29,626 --> 00:07:32,889
Both are quiet, both are far, and neither can pull on us.

121
00:07:32,889 --> 00:07:35,979
To fall into a black hole, you would have to go to it.

122
00:07:36,679 --> 00:07:39,456
So, what happens if you fall into a black hole?

123
00:07:39,456 --> 00:07:42,824
Pick a small one, and you're spaghetti before you arrive.

124
00:07:42,824 --> 00:07:51,036
Pick a giant, and you cross in peace, with a minute, or a day, left on your watch, while your friends watch you freeze forever at the edge.

125
00:07:51,036 --> 00:07:53,931
But black holes aren't the only giants out there.

126
00:07:53,931 --> 00:07:59,721
Some stars are so big that, placed where our Sun is, they'd swallow every planet out past Jupiter.

127
00:07:59,721 --> 00:08:01,553
So just how big can a star get?

128
00:08:01,553 --> 00:08:05,452
Next, we put the largest star ever measured right next to our Sun.
